WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.233
Unit testing basics.

2
00:00:02.533 --> 00:00:05.433
This habit separates engineers from notebook users.

3
00:00:06.633 --> 00:00:09.000
Every unit test has the same skeleton.

4
00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:11.067
Arrange: set up the inputs.

5
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:13.133
Act: run the code under test.

6
00:00:13.433 --> 00:00:14.900
Assert: check the result.

7
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.733
Good tests share three traits.

8
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:21.167
They're isolated, with no shared state between tests.

9
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:25.833
They're deterministic: no live network, and no randomness without a seed.

10
00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:28.867
And they're named after the behavior they pin down.

11
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:34.200
Test the contract, not the implementation, and always cover the edges.

12
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.133
Take a hazard score function.

13
00:00:36.433 --> 00:00:39.833
A normal case: a big, close asteroid scores high.

14
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:43.567
An edge case: zero diameter scores exactly zero.

15
00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:47.400
And an error case: a negative distance must raise an error.

16
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.967
Here they are in code, using the built-in unittest module.

17
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:54.133
The first checks the normal case.

18
00:00:54.433 --> 00:00:56.733
The second pins down the zero edge case.

19
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:01.367
The third uses assertRaises to demand a ValueError for bad input.

20
00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.533
A real case.

21
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:13.433
Your RAG chunker has three testable contracts: never exceed max tokens, never split mid-sentence, and return an empty list for an empty document.

22
00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:19.367
The week those tests were skipped, a whitespace edge case silently corrupted twelve thousand chunks.

23
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:24.200
Retrieval bugs are far harder to see downstream than a red test up front.

24
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:26.251
Let's recap.

25
00:01:26.251 --> 00:01:28.733
Every test is arrange, act, assert.

26
00:01:29.033 --> 00:01:32.333
Keep tests isolated, deterministic, and well named.

27
00:01:32.633 --> 00:01:38.467
Test the contract, not the implementation, and always cover zero, empty, and invalid input.

28
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:42.133
Next up: pytest, which makes all of this much nicer.
